<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David of Trebizond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1408–<a href="/years/1463">1463</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as David Megas Komnenos · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">David of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> was the last emperor of Trebizond, reigning from 1459. He sought a Christian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but in 1461 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> besieged Trebizond and David surrendered it, ending the last Byzantine successor state. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and executed with his sons in 1463.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>David Megas Komnenos, sometimes numbered David II, was the third son of Emperor Alexios IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and Theodora Kantakouzene. Throughout the reign of his older brother John IV he held the courtly title of despotes, which in Trebizond marked the heir to the throne. He took part in John's expeditions against the Genoese and carried out diplomatic tasks. In 1458 he ratified his brother's treaty with the Ottoman Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> at Adrianople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>David took the throne shortly after John's death. John had named his nephew Alexios V as heir, but Alexios was a minor, and according to Laonikos Chalkokondyles David, backed by the Kabasitanoi archontes, pushed him aside. John had spent his last years building alliances with the Georgian princes and Uzun Hassan, and David seems to have counted on them. Michael Alighieri, who said he was David's envoy, negotiated a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> that gave the city a consulate and a 2% levy on exports, as the Genoese and Venetians enjoyed.</p><p>Before Western support was firm, David asked Mehmed to remit the tribute paid by his predecessor, using the envoys of Uzun Hassan, who made arrogant demands of their own. Mehmed sent a fleet along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and led an army east, taking Sinope. When Uzun Hassan's mother, Sara Khatun, made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> lost its strongest ally. The fleet reached the city in early July 1461 and besieged it for over a month. His protovestiarios George Amiroutzes advised David to sue for peace, and David surrendered on 15 August 1461, on condition that he keep his family, household and wealth, with the promise of a profitable retirement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>David was settled at Adrianople with estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Struma</a> valley, but Mehmed saw him as a possible rallying point for Greek resistance. Letters said to be from his niece Theodora, asking that one of his sons or his nephew Alexios be sent to her, gave the excuse; Theodore Spandounes claims they were forged on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s orders. David, his three sons and Alexios were imprisoned on 26 March 1463 and executed with the sword on 1 November 1463 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His surrender marks the end of the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and of the Byzantine imperial tradition. In July 2013 David, his sons and his nephew were canonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a> of the Patriarchate of Constantinople, with a feast day on 1 November.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “David of Trebizond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1443" aria-label="Trabzon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of David of Trebizond"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1443">What was here in 1443? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
